    "instruction": "Implement an accessible accordion where each header is a button toggling aria-expanded and controlling a panel with aria-controls.",
    "output": "<style>\n  .acc { max-width: 560px; border: 1px solid #e2e8f0; border-radius: 10px; overflow: hidden; }\n  .acc-item + .acc-item { border-top: 1px solid #e2e8f0; }\n  .acc-trigger {\n    width: 100%; display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: center;\n    padding: .9rem 1rem; background: #fff; border: none; cursor: pointer;\n    font-weight: 600; text-align: left; color: #0f172a;\n  }\n  .acc-trigger:hover { background: #f8fafc; }\n  .acc-trigger:focus-visible { outline: 3px solid #2563eb; outline-offset: -3px; }\n  .acc-panel { padding: 0 1rem 1rem; color: #475569; font-size: .95rem; }\n  .acc-panel[hidden] { display: none; }\n</style>\n<div class=\"acc\">\n  <div class=\"acc-item\">\n    <h3 style=\"margin:0;\">\n      <button class=\"acc-trigger\" type=\"button\" aria-expanded=\"true\" aria-controls=\"panel-1\" id=\"trigger-1\">\n        What does aria-expanded do?<span aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8722;</span>\n      </button>\n    </h3>\n    <div class=\"acc-panel\" id=\"panel-1\" role=\"region\" aria-labelledby=\"trigger-1\">\n      It tells assistive tech whether the controlled region is open or closed.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"acc-item\">\n    <h3 style=\"margin:0;\">\n      <button class=\"acc-trigger\" type=\"button\" aria-expanded=\"false\" aria-controls=\"panel-2\" id=\"trigger-2\">\n        Why use real buttons?<span aria-hidden=\"true\">+</span>\n      </button>\n    </h3>\n    <div class=\"acc-panel\" id=\"panel-2\" role=\"region\" aria-labelledby=\"trigger-2\" hidden>\n      Buttons are natively keyboard operable and announce their role.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>"
  },
